  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the presence or absence of a sure outcome in the lotteries. However, in this case, neither lottery has a sure outcome, so the reason does not clearly favor one over the other. The individual did not refer to certainty or sure outcomes at all in their reasoning. Instead, their justification was based on minimizing regret in the face of near-certain loss, preferring the higher prize when both are unlikely. This mode of thinking is unrelated to the structural feature (presence of a sure outcome) that the decision reason evaluates. Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual applied the decision reason. The information considered by the individual (probabilities and prize magnitudes under low likelihood) does not align with the information relevant to the reason (existence of a sure outcome). The justification (go for the bigger prize since winning is unlikely anyway) is also unrelated to the logic of avoiding sure outcomes. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
